{"subjectId":"sql","id":"window-functions","title":"Window Functions: The Basics","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":36,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A window function computes a value <strong>across a set of related rows</strong> — without collapsing them like <code>GROUP BY</code> does. Each row keeps its identity and gains an aggregate, a rank or a neighbour's value.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>OVER clause</strong> — <code>f(…) OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY … frame)</code> — <code>PARTITION BY</code> splits rows into independent groups, <code>ORDER BY</code> orders rows inside each partition, the frame picks which rows around the current one are included.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>GROUP BY vs window</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- GROUP BY: one row per department\nSELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS dept_avg\nFROM employees WHERE dept_id IN (2, 3)\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;\n\n-- window: every employee kept, department average attached\nSELECT name, dept_id, salary,\n       AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) AS dept_avg,\n       salary - AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) AS diff\nFROM employees WHERE dept_id IN (2, 3)\nORDER BY dept_id, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | dept_avg\n--------+---------\n2       | 84000.0\n3       | 82500.0\n\nname   | dept_id | salary | dept_avg | diff\n-------+---------+--------+----------+---------\nPriya  | 2       | 110000 | 84000.0  | 26000.0\nArjun  | 2       | 70000  | 84000.0  | -14000.0\nNeha   | 2       | 72000  | 84000.0  | -12000.0\nVikram | 3       | 80000  | 82500.0  | -2500.0\nSara   | 3       | 85000  | 82500.0  | 2500.0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sum\"></i>Aggregates as window functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, dept_id, salary,\n       SUM(salary)   OVER ()                     AS company_payroll,\n       ROUND(100.0 * salary / SUM(salary) OVER (), 1) AS pct_of_company,\n       COUNT(*)      OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) AS dept_size,\n       MAX(salary)   OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) AS dept_max\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY dept_id, salary DESC, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | dept_id | salary | company_payroll | pct_of_company | dept_size | dept_max\n-------+---------+--------+-----------------+----------------+-----------+---------\nAsha   | 1       | 150000 | 877000          | 17.1           | 4         | 150000\nRavi   | 1       | 120000 | 877000          | 13.7           | 4         | 150000\nMeera  | 1       | 95000  | 877000          | 10.8           | 4         | 150000\nKaran  | 1       | 95000  | 877000          | 10.8           | 4         | 150000\nPriya  | 2       | 110000 | 877000          | 12.5           | 3         | 110000\nNeha   | 2       | 72000  | 877000          | 8.2            | 3         | 110000\nArjun  | 2       | 70000  | 877000          | 8.0            | 3         | 110000\nSara   | 3       | 85000  | 877000          | 9.7            | 2         | 85000\nVikram | 3       | 80000  | 877000          | 9.1            | 2         | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>OVER ()</code> with nothing inside = one window containing all rows (of the result after <code>WHERE</code>).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trending-up\"></i>Adding ORDER BY makes it cumulative</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT day, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day)  AS running_total,\n       COUNT(*)    OVER (ORDER BY day)  AS days_so_far\nFROM daily_sales\nORDER BY day;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>day        | amount | running_total | days_so_far\n-----------+--------+---------------+------------\n2024-03-01 | 120    | 120           | 1\n2024-03-02 | 80     | 200           | 2\n2024-03-03 | 150    | 350           | 3\n2024-03-04 | 90     | 440           | 4\n2024-03-05 | 200    | 640           | 5\n2024-03-06 | 60     | 700           | 6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>With <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code>, the default frame becomes “from the start of the partition up to the current row”, so <code>SUM</code> turns into a running total. Details in <em>Running Totals &amp; Window Frames</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>The family</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Functions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aggregate</td><td><code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>COUNT</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code> + <code>OVER (…)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ranking</td><td><code>ROW_NUMBER</code>, <code>RANK</code>, <code>DENSE_RANK</code>, <code>NTILE</code>, <code>PERCENT_RANK</code>, <code>CUME_DIST</code></td></tr><tr><td>Offset / value</td><td><code>LAG</code>, <code>LEAD</code>, <code>FIRST_VALUE</code>, <code>LAST_VALUE</code>, <code>NTH_VALUE</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-map-pin\"></i>Where you can use them</h3>\n<p>Window functions are evaluated in the <code>SELECT</code> step, so they are allowed only in <code>SELECT</code> and <code>ORDER BY</code>. To filter on one, compute it in a CTE/subquery first:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name FROM employees\nWHERE salary &gt; AVG(salary) OVER ();</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>misuse of window function AVG()</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH x AS (\n    SELECT name, salary, AVG(salary) OVER () AS avg_all FROM employees\n)\nSELECT name, salary FROM x WHERE salary &gt; avg_all ORDER BY salary DESC, name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary\n------+-------\nAsha  | 150000\nRavi  | 120000\nPriya | 110000\nKaran | 95000\nMeera | 95000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Snowflake / BigQuery / DuckDB (QUALIFY)</figcaption><pre><code>-- Snowflake / BigQuery / DuckDB / Databricks: filter on a window directly\nSELECT name, dept_id, salary\nFROM employees\nQUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) = 1;</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between GROUP BY and a window function?</summary><div><p><code>GROUP BY</code> collapses each group into one output row, so you lose row detail. A window function computes over a group (the partition) but returns a value on <strong>every</strong> row — so you can show each employee next to their department average, rank or running total.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is PARTITION BY?</summary><div><p>The window equivalent of <code>GROUP BY</code>: it splits rows into independent partitions, and the window function restarts for each one. Without it, the whole result set is a single partition.</p></div></details></section>"}